.title = "Colophon",
.description = "Exceptionally boring technology, by design",
.date = .date("2025-01-01T00:00:00"),
.layout = "plain.shtml",
---

> []($block.attrs('hi'))
>
> This colophon[^colophon] addresses the design principles that drive the
> technical implementation of this blog.
>
> In a few words, it isn't built like one of these modern Web blobs throwing
> heaps of heterogeneous solutions at perceptual problems.

# [Not much of anything]($section.id('not-much-of-anything'))

The production site is static HTML and CSS. It has no database, application
server, hydration process, client-side router, analytics bundle, or JavaScript
framework.

The source is compiled by three deliberately boring command-line tools:

- [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) describes and orchestrates the build;
- [Sass](https://sass-lang.com/) compiles the existing SCSS design into one CSS
  asset; and
- [Zine](https://zine-ssg.io/) validates and renders SuperMD content through
  structurally checked SuperHTML layouts.

There is **no JavaScript required for interaction**. The drop-down navigation,
links and back-references, collapsible table of contents, responsive layout,
dark mode, and reduced-motion behaviour use HTML and CSS.

And there are effectively **no pictures**. Text works quite well to convey
information, although the design does indulge in a few tiny SVG masks and local
Web fonts.

# [Uncompromising simplicity]($section.id('uncompromising-simplicity'))

The generated website employs a single CSS document, and each article is a
single HTML page. The rest are a few fonts optimised for Web performance using
the [Web Open Font Format](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_Open_Font_Format).

The CSS has no rounded corners and no animation framework. The source styles
remain split into focused Sass partials, while browsers receive one compiled
`base.css` file.

Zine performs syntax highlighting at build time and validates internal page and
fragment links. SuperHTML rejects malformed template structure instead of
treating templates as arbitrary text concatenation.

# [But the user experience?]($section.id('but-the-user-experience'))

The site remains responsive from mobile to desktop and adapts to dark and light
modes. It uses semantically grounded [View
Transitions](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/View_Transition_API)
where available and degrades gracefully where they are not.

Content remains usable with JavaScript disabled because there is no JavaScript
to disable.

# [Some room for improvement]($section.id('room-for-improvement'))

The Zine migration is still under way. A representative set of flight rules,
flashcards, and ramblings has been ported, while the remaining Zola content is
kept intact as the reference implementation.

The largest remaining jobs are completing the content conversion, implementing
the taxonomy and Atom feed generators, and auditing subtle styling differences
between Zola's Markdown output and SuperMD's output.

[^colophon]: A [colophon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colophon_(publishing))
    is a piece of content sharing meta-information about the document it
    annotates. Examples are found as far back as antiquity, on clay tablets.
